== 14. Of the Son

The son is he who is in subjection to his parents.

The duties a son is to perform to his parents are chiefly two.

#emph[First, to yield them obedience, whether they are his natural parents or otherwise, as his stepfather and stepmother, and that as long as he lives.] Eph 6:1: “Children, obey your#super[+] parents in the Lord, for this is right.” Luke 2:51: “Then He went down to Nazareth with them and was obedient to them.” Exod 18:19: Moses’ father-in-law said to him, “Now listen to me; I will give you some advice, and may God be with you.” Ruth 3:5: Ruth said to Naomi her mother-in-law, “I will do everything you say.” Job 1:5: “And when the days of feasting were over, Job would send for his children to purify them.”

This obedience must show itself in two things.

First, in the choice of a lawful calling, in which the child is to be directed and appointed at the discretion of the parent.

Secondly, in marriage. For in that the parent is the chief agent and disposer of the child. Now his authority is not so great that the child is to be forced and compelled by him. Yet the reverent and dutiful respect which the child ought to bear toward him ought to be a strong inducement not to dissent from his advice, or reject it, without great and weighty cause. Indeed, the child must endeavor by every kind of dutiful conduct to overcome, or at least to soften, his parents’ severity in that matter.

Now for the daughters: they must yield obedience to their parents in all household labors, so that they may be skillful in household affairs. So the seven daughters of the priest of Midian accustomed themselves to draw water and fill the troughs “to water their father’s flock” (Exod 2:16). So Rebekah was seen to come out of her father’s house with her jar on her shoulder, and to go down with it to the well to fill it, and to give drink to her father’s camels (Gen 24:16, 17).

The second duty of the son is to repay his father’s love and care over him by relieving him in time of want, if God gives the ability, with food and clothing and other necessities. In this, children must deal with their parents as the brood of the stork is reported to deal with her, feeding her when she is old; and in doing so they do no more than what she did for them before. It is Paul’s counsel that children and grandchildren should repay the kindness of their kindred in the first place (1 Tim 5:4); and therefore of their parents, the head and foundation of their kindred. When Joseph was in prosperity and his father Jacob in want, he first gave him grain freely, and afterward sent for him to Egypt and provided for him there. So much so that the text says of him that he “provided his father and brothers and all his father’s household with food,” even as the mother puts food into the child’s mouth (Gen 47:12). When Naomi, Ruth’s mother-in-law, was of great age and her strength was spent, Ruth gathered grain in the harvest for the relief of them both. Indeed, when Boaz gave her food and drink, she kept back part of her meal and brought it home with her gleanings to refresh her mother-in-law (Ruth 2:14, 18).

The necessity of performing these duties is so great that if the son neglects them and does the contrary, by ill-treating his parent either in word or deed, he is worthy of death, even by Moses’ law. Exod 21:15: “Whoever strikes his father or mother must surely be put to death.”

The law of the firstborn among a man’s children, that he should have a greater portion of his father’s goods than any of the rest of his brothers, is perpetual. It admits no exception, but only in the case of notorious wickedness. Deut 21:17: “He must acknowledge the firstborn, the son of his unloved wife, by giving him a double portion of all that he has. For that son is the firstfruits of his father’s strength; the right of the firstborn belongs to him.” This was the law. Yet we read that Reuben lost this prerogative because of his incest, and Judah, the fourth son of Jacob, had the dignity of the firstborn. Gen 49:8: “Judah, your brothers shall praise you. … Your father’s sons shall bow down to you.”

In the Church of the Old Testament the privilege of the firstborn was threefold: the first of government and royalty, the second of priesthood, the third of the double portion. And it is probable that this was for the most part ceremonial, and was fulfilled and ended in Christ, who is “the image of the invisible God, the firstborn over all creation” (Col 1:15).

Here two questions are put forward.

#emph[Question I.] Is it lawful for the son to make a vow concerning either religion or civil life without the consent of the parent?

#emph[Answer.] A vow so made has no force to bind the child, but by the law of nature and common equity it is to be made void. For it is a settled case that he who is not his own man (that is to say, not at his own liberty, but under the power of another) cannot bind himself. Again, the authority of the parent by the law of God is great. For it allows the father to make void the vow of the child concerning God’s worship (Num 30:5).

#emph[Question II.] Is the consent of the parents to be required in the second marriages of their children?

#emph[Answer.] 1. In ancient times it was provided, both by #footnote[C. de Nuptijs. L. vidua, & L. in coniunctione.] civil laws and also by imperial constitutions, that no widows, though made free by their former marriages, should make any contract again without the goodwill of their parents. 2. Consent in this case, though it is not #emph[absolutely necessary,] is yet to be thought #emph[fit and proper.] For children who are in some respects exempted from their parents’ authority still owe them the duty of honor, by God’s express commandment (Exod 20:12). And so they ought to show it by being directed and advised by them in some measure, even when they give themselves in marriage a second time. That is why#footnote[Beza de repud. & diuort. sect. de sponsalibus absque consensu parentum factis.] later divines have held, on good ground, that the espousals of widows, who have somewhat more liberty than a child married for the first time, cannot escape just blame if they are made without their parents’ knowledge, however much they may stand in force without it.
